findings.description:
Line 89 says delete "mirrors write — deletes from whichever project file the key's scope maps to". For `git.integration_branch` that file is always `.context-forge.local.toml`. Yet line 146 and walkthrough step 9 (line 259) tell the user to clear the stale shared copy with `cf config unset git.integration_branch --project`. Under the design's own routing, that command:
- deletes the developer's **personal** value (`legacy/value`), which is the opposite of the intent;
- leaves the stale shared copy in place, so `get` now falls through to `another-legacy-value` (Data Flow step 2);
- leaves the `cf check` warning in place.

After a collision skip, no command can remove the shared copy. Only hand-editing `.context-forge.toml` ends that state. `unset` is also a regression for every existing installation. Today `ConfigManager.delete` (`packages/core/src/config/ConfigManager.ts:187-204`) removes the key from `.context-forge.toml`. After this slice, `cf config unset git.integration_branch --project` on a pre-migration project prints `Unset git.integration_branch (project)` (`packages/cli/src/commands/config.ts:148`), but the value still resolves from the shared file.

Fix: define project-scope `delete` for a personal key to remove it from **both** project files. Or give `migrate-personal` a flag that drops stale shared copies. Then update line 146 and step 9 to match.

The parent architecture requires "No behavior changes without tests" (900-arch principle). Line 222 lists `migrate-personal` tests for the happy path and no-op only. These behaviours have no named tests:
- collision skip (different values) and identical-value delete (line 146);
- continuing after a per-key failure and reporting `failed:` (line 149);
- the new `cf init` gitignore create/append/no-duplicate behaviour (line 159);
- project-scope `unset` routing for a personal key (line 89).

These are exactly where the FAIL above lives. Tests for them would have exposed it.

`git.integration_branch` is the only personal key (line 111). Even so, the design adds:
- a dedicated subcommand;
- three-branch collision semantics;
- per-key continue-on-failure with `moved` / `skipped` / `failed` reporting;
- a checker rule.

A lighter design meets the slice plan's "defined migration/compat story":
- keep the read-time fallback and the `cf check` warning;
- when `set(personalKey, …, 'project')` runs, also remove the key from the shared file (this also resolves the collision case naturally);
- make project-scope `unset` of a personal key clear both files.

With that, `migrate-personal`, the collision rule and the per-key failure reporting can be cut or deferred. If the dedicated command stays, at minimum drop the per-key continuation and reporting framework (line 149).
